course: dictionary
lesson: 9746
title: Selling words and what they mean
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Adverts and magazines often use special words to make things sound better than they are. Read the words around them to spot the trick. «"Superfood" is a selling word, not a science word; blueberries are healthy, but no single food cures diseases.» The clue is "selling word": a superfood is a food that people say is extra healthy, and the word is used to sell food.
explain: Check the meaning with the swap test. Put "very healthy food" in place of superfood and see if the sentence still makes sense. It does, so you know the word means a food that people say is extra healthy. Then ask: does the sentence say it is magic? It does not, so the word is not a science term.
explain: Even a healthy food is not magic. «Spinach contains iron, but the body absorbs it poorly, so spinach is not the iron superfood of cartoons.» The word "but" is the clue: spinach has iron, yet the body does not take it in well. A balanced diet, with lots of different foods, matters most.
fact: A superfood is a food that people say is extra healthy because it has lots of vitamins, minerals or other good things in it.
fact: Blueberries, kale and salmon are often called superfoods.
fact: "Superfood" is mostly used in adverts and magazines, not in science.
fact: No single food keeps you healthy on its own; a balanced diet matters most.
fact: Words like "superfood" can make food sound magical so that it sells for more.
exercise: «"Superfood" is a selling word, not a science word.» What is a superfood? | *A food that people say is extra healthy because it has lots of vitamins and minerals | A food that cures every illness | A food that is always sold in a box | A food that only grows in very hot countries
exercise: Why is «superfood» called a selling word? | It is a word that scientists use in every lab | It only appears in maths books | *It is mostly used in adverts and magazines, not in science
exercise: «Spinach contains iron, but the body absorbs it poorly.» Find and copy one word that means takes in. | *absorbs | contains | spinach | iron
test: «Blueberries are healthy, but no single food cures diseases.» What does this sentence tell us? | Blueberries cure every disease | Scientists never eat fruit | Blueberries are unhealthy and should never be eaten | *Even very healthy foods cannot cure diseases on their own
test: What is a trick that adverts can use with words like «superfood»? | *They make a food sound magical so that people buy more of it | They ask you to read a textbook | They tell you the truth about every food | They use long science words that nobody understands
exam: Ella says, «A balanced diet matters more than eating one superfood.» Is she right? | No, superfoods are science words that cure illness | No, one food can keep you healthy forever | Yes, because adverts are always correct | *Yes, no single food can keep you healthy on its own
